WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.667
-Thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.234
-Thank you for having me.
I'm on "The Tonight Show."

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:05.334
I'm kind of freaking out.
Hi, Mom.

00:00:05.334 --> 00:00:07.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. This is exciting.

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:10.200
You're joining MSNBC pretty
soon, in the fall.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.367
I mean, we're gonna be
neighbors.

00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:12.968
-We are.
-Yeah, congratulations on --

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.267
-Do you have any tips for me?

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.100
-No. I have none.
For you? No.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.534
You tell me how to do it,
please.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:19.634
Congrats. Are you excited?
Are you nervous?

00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:20.901
Do you know
what you're gonna do?

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:22.400
-I'm so excited.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.868
I mean, there's
so much going on in the world

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.200
it turns out, right?
-Yeah.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.934
-Lot of news to talk about.
-Exactly.

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:28.601
-And I'm gonna be joining MSNBC

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:31.067
at a time where there's
a big midterm election,

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.601
lot of things to explain
to the American people,

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.300
and joining this great team
that's already doing that.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.267
So, I'm excited. I'm thrilled.

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:39.767
I'm ready to get started after I
have a little time to simmer.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.667
-Yeah. When will you start?
Around -- Do we have a date?

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:43.734
September?
Somewhere around there?

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:46.033
-I'm starting in September.
-Great.

00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:49.467
-And, again, midterm elections.
Exciting time. Lots going on.

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:51.934
-You know, you just --
you just wrapped up your job

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:55.634
as President Biden's White House
Press Secretary two weeks ago.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:56.934
-I did.
-I have to say,

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.067
you did a fantastic job.
-Thank you.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.200
-Really great job, and that must
be a tough, tough gig.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.934
-It is.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:07.968
The secret is that it's the best
job I've ever had to date.

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:11.234
And I loved it.
I mean, if I were to wake up --

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.534
Sometimes I have these
nightmares --

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:13.601
I don't know
if this happens to you

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.033
where you're a surgeon

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.868
or you have to do brain surgery
or something like that

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:19.167
and I wake up in a cold sweat.

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:21.767
If I had to do something like
that, I would freak out.

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:23.901
But being at the podium,

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:27.601
explaining difficult things to
the public, that was fun to me.

00:01:27.601 --> 00:01:29.534
It was great. I learned
something new every day.

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:33.000
I got to call people up
who were the experts on things.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.467
Jake Sullivan and Brian Deese
and all these people

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:38.133
who knew so much, and I learned
something new every day.

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:40.534
It was fantastic.
-I wanted to get your

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:44.267
perspective on the catastrophic
mass school shooting

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:45.467
that happened yesterday.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:48.000
Obviously
we're all shaken by this,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.734
but how does the White House
handle events of this type?

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:53.934
-Yeah, I was --
I have two little kids.

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:55.434
I know you have kids
I think that are

00:01:55.434 --> 00:01:57.567
a little older than my kids.
-Second and third grade.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:58.667
-Second and third grade.

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:00.767
Mine are in kindergarten
and preschool.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:04.400
And the first reaction I had
when I saw the news

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.834
was shock, of course,

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:07.467
and I'm sure
this is what they were feeling

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.033
in the White House too.

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.367
And sadness.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.400
You know, that kind of sadness
that you get

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.634
when your throat
feels like it's --

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.234
if you talk you're gonna cry,
that kind of sadness.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:21.000
And then you feel fear.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.167
And I think like so many parents
across the country,

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.667
I was thinking about sending my
daughter to kindergarten today

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.133
and is she safe,
is she okay there,

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.334
what kind of security do they
have, should they have security.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:34.400
And those
are the kind of thoughts

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.434
that are going through your head

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:36.901
when you're in the White House
too,

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:40.367
because you're thinking about
what the country's experiencing,

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.234
what communities
are experiencing,

00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:45.701
and what you can do
to help heal,

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:48.801
help bring some calm if you can
in that moment.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.901
-Did you hear President Biden's
remarks?

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:52.300
-I did. I did.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.300
Did you watch them last night?
-Yes, I did. Yeah.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.567
-I did, and I think
what people saw who watched

00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.133
or saw clips later on

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:03.067
was somebody who knows
what it's like to lose a child

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:07.033
and knows what it's like
to have part of your soul

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.534
and part of
your heart ripped out,

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.801
and that's what he talked about
a little bit last night.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:12.601
And watching it,
I was thinking --

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.534
I was looking at his face
and watching

00:03:14.534 --> 00:03:17.801
kind of the crinkles in his eyes
and the emotions,

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:20.000
and I was looking at Dr. Biden
behind him

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.734
and thinking these
are people who know that pain.

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:25.100
It doesn't make it heal it
for other people,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.033
but they understand what it is

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:30.634
and it impacts how they live
their lives and how they govern.

00:03:30.634 --> 00:03:34.334
-And do you think that is going
to do something finally?

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:38.033
Are people going to -- Is it
change -- Is change coming?

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:42.133
What can people do besides vote
and volunteer?

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.400
I mean, what do you think?
It's awful.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.267
-This is a common question
that people ask me,

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:50.100
and I think the most important
thing for people to know is

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.968
nobody's powerless in this
moment.

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:54.300
Everybody has a voice.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.801
Everybody can use that voice
in many ways.

00:03:56.801 --> 00:03:58.534
Yes, you can vote.

00:03:58.534 --> 00:04:00.567
You can also get involved
in a lot of organizations.

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:02.434
There are amazing organizations

00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:04.434
fighting for gun reform
out there.

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:06.634
And that's another way
to get involved.

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:08.901
And nobody is powerless.

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:11.000
What I would also say that I
learned from

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.100
working in the White House
and working in government

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.100
is that you have to
always have hope.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.801
You have to always think that
change is possible

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:22.801
and that maybe it's not gonna
happen next month or next year,

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:25.100
I don't know,
but what happens when you have

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.167
tragedies like the one in Texas

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.000
is it jerks people awake
in a lot of ways.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.000
I mean, guns are the biggest --
they kill more kids every year

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:34.934
than anything else.

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:36.000
That's a fact.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.434
There are more guns
in this country

00:04:37.434 --> 00:04:39.634
than there are people
in this country,

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:42.534
and those type of details
jerk people awake,

00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:44.701
and that's sometimes
when change happens.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.901
-Yeah. I appreciate
you giving us your thoughts.

00:04:46.901 --> 00:04:50.234
Thank you so much, and good luck
on your new gig over at MSNBC.

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:52.133
-I appreciate it.
-Jen Psaki, everybody.

